      existed but were unreachable. (a70ffa0)
- [x] :fold close-other [regex] / zx. (1fd0f55)
- [x] item 1 — :save is a snapshot; streaming is opt-in via --follow or the
      final [w]rite-once / [f]ollow prompt step. (e3da1dd)
- [x] item 2 — :saveconfig renamed to :save-config, no alias. (e9e9daf)
- [x] item 3 — Events header keeps its key hints; log path collapses $HOME to ~
      then elides from the left. (9d43a86)
- [x] item 4 — :help takes a tab (number 1-6, name, prefix, next/prev);
      :help-tab dropped; ? opens Bindings; :man alias; toggles return to the
      origin tab. (e7f6265)
- [x] item 5 — overlay nav keys are rebindable: the trie is consulted before
      the shared nav handler. Arrows-switch-tabs idea dropped as requested.
      (798dc99)
- [x] item 7 — markup-aware slicing; the tab header and colour-annotated
      legend lines are no longer over-cut. (2951235)
- [x] CLAUDE.md corrected: the 'A toggles all-bindings' and '_mode_bindings'
      claims were both stale.
- [x] save prompt completes paths with Tab / Shift-Tab (LCP, then cycle, popup
      shared with the ':' line; directories keep a trailing '/'). (9f869df)
- [x] entering a directory (e.g. '.') no longer passes the exists() check and
      ask truncate/append about something unwritable — rejected with a reason,
      prompt stays open. Guard sits in the single write path so ':save <dir>'
      is covered too. (9f869df)
- [x] save prompt scrolls to keep the end of a long path visible, leading '…'
      marks the hidden head. (28f5456)
- [x] ':' command line scrolls the same way — completion there was correct but
      invisible, so Tab-cycling a long path looked inert. Window follows the
      cursor, so Home/End move the view. (b91f27b)
  - STILL CLIPPED: the search and filter prompts. Same one-line fix via
    _scroll_text_window; left alone because regex queries are usually short.
  - Note: unit tests cannot catch this class of bug — they assert on the
    buffer, which was always right. Only integration tests see the clipping.

## Late-ICMP fix (2026-08-18) — see doc/report-late-icmp-x-pattern.md

- [x] DONE option A, preventive variant. 'ping -O' means "no answer yet", so a
      '-O' line now parks the icmp_seq in Monitor._pending instead of counting a
      loss. The probe resolves exactly once: _record_reply (late answer, cell
      drawn 'x' yellow) or _expire_pending (write-off after :set late-grace,
      default 1s, driven by the existing 0.5s state loop).
      Fixed: X.X.X. bar, Loss% 50%->0%, tx double-count, badge flapping,
      '-  -' Up/Down columns, greyed stats, clock-probe re-arm churn.
      Verified with a fake `ping` on PATH emitting the report's exact pair:
        before  .X.X.X.  Loss 50.0%  RX 13 TX 26 XX 13  Up/Down '-'
        after   xxxxxxx  Loss  0.0%  RX 13 TX 13 XX  0  Up/Down 14s
      Cost accepted: a genuinely dead host is written off ~late-grace later
      (X at ~2s instead of ~1s; the logged event was already delayed 3s).

- [x] Fixed in passing: autofold, autofold-delay, clock-interval and late-grace
      were all initialised BELOW the _load_config() call, so a value set in the
      config file was immediately overwritten by the default — those three
      settings had never worked from a config file. Moved above it, next to the
      existing "must be set before _load_config" block. 4 regression tests.

- [ ] NOT done: option B, per-host monitor ping options
      (`:ping-options *-cam* -i 2`). Deliberately deferred — it hides the
      condition A now reports. If picked up, these bite:
        - SshPingMonitor._STALE_SECS = 2.0 synthesises a lost ping when the SSH
          pipe is quiet >2s ("slightly above the 1s ping interval"). With -i 2
          that fires on EVERY probe; _STALE_SECS must derive from the effective
          interval.
        - history-size is documented as "24h at 1 ping/s"; a per-host -i
          silently breaks that window for those hosts.
        - a runtime change only takes effect on the next Popen, which today
          happens only on subprocess exit or :edit — needs a monitor-restart
          path that does not exist.
        - do the rules lookup LAZILY inside _build_ping_cmd: monitors are
          constructed during the entry loop but threads start afterwards, so a
          lazy lookup is order-independent while an eager one would force
          :ping-options to precede the host lines.

- [ ] Still open from the report's §"Secondary observation": rtime/drift show
      no-rt for those cams. Most likely genuine lack of ICMP-timestamp support
      rather than a window miss (the probe uses -W 2, ample for 1.2s). The flap
      that re-armed probing on every DOWN->UP is gone now, so revisit only if
      no-rt persists on a host known to answer -T.

## Wedged ping / stderr deadlock (2026-08-18)

- [x] DONE root cause: both monitors read only stdout and drained stderr after
      the loop — i.e. never, for a process started without -w.  The child filled
      the stderr pipe (8 KiB here, ~200 per-probe error lines, ~3.5 min), blocked
      in write(2,...), stopped pinging AND stopped writing stdout while the parent
      waited on stdout.  strace on the NAS showed  write(2, "ping: ", 6.
      Symptom: frozen row; in sync-history mode, one blank per second.
- [x] Both loops hoisted into SubprocessMonitor: select() on BOTH pipes, raw
      os.read + own line assembly (select cannot see a line already buffered in a
      TextIOWrapper — the ssh path had that latent bug).  PortMonitor unaffected.
- [x] Local path gained the staleness detection it never had, so a stuck child
      shows as losses rather than freezing.
- [x] Watchdog: after _STALE_MAX_WINDOWS (3) silent windows the child is
      terminated and respawned; logs "ping-bulk: no output for Ns — restarting
      ping" once, and that text becomes the reason on the host-down event.
      Gated on prior output so a slow ssh connect is not cut short.
      Verified live: ...XXX...XXX...XXX... cycle, RX climbing 3->7->12 instead of
      freezing, exactly one child alive after 45s of cycles (no leak/zombies).
- [x] Fatal classification stays post-exit AND only for a run that produced no
      result line.  'network is unreachable' is in _FATAL_PING_ERRORS but ping
      prints it per probe while working; condemning a host for it is permanent
      because self.error has no reset path.  A review caught that my first cut
      still got this wrong.
- [x] Also fixed: stale-window loss now consumes an outstanding icmp_seq, else
      _expire_pending counted the same probe twice and inflated Loss%.
- [x] Test harness reworked to real os.pipe() pairs.  The old MagicMock harness
      used fatal stderr as the loop terminator, so no test ever exercised a live
      stderr stream — which is why this survived 1600 tests.  The flood test
      times out against the old code.

- [ ] STILL OPEN: monitor.error is a one-way trapdoor — 6 setters, no reset
      anywhere.  Not needed while classification stays post-exit, but it is the
      reason the safe fix had to be conservative.
- [ ] STILL OPEN: _edit_reload_inplace calls t.join(timeout=0), a no-op; old
      threads are abandoned and rely on running=False + terminate().
- [ ] STILL OPEN: a child that never produces any output (e.g. ssh hanging on
      connect) is not watchdogged — deliberately, to avoid killing a slow
      connect.  A separate, longer first-output deadline would close it.

## STILL OPEN from this review
- Pinned overlay rows ignore h_scroll, and max_line_w excludes them, so a
  header wider than the box cannot be scrolled into view. It now degrades at
  token boundaries instead of mid-label, which was the reported symptom, but
  the underlying asymmetry remains.
- _build_tab_lines() re-shifts _settings_line_map by _HELP_PINNED on every
  call and is called at least twice per interaction; idempotent only because
  _build_settings_lines rebuilds from scratch. Still fragile.
- The settings-cursor highlight row draws with addstr and len(), so a settings
  line containing markup would highlight the wrong span. Not hit today.

## Help overlay: tab 5 [Bindings] shows no reference text (2026-09-08)

Tab 4 [Commands] pairs a command list with its syntax reference; tab 5
[Bindings] shows only `_build_all_bindings_lines()` — the live table of what
is bound right now — and no help at all.  So the answer to "how do I bind
this?" is on a different tab from the bindings themselves.

The material already exists: `_HELP_BINDINGS` (ping-bulk:945) documents
`:bind-key` / `:unbind-key`, the key notation, the `%` variables and the `\;`
separator.  It is currently included on the Interactive and Hosts tabs
(_build_tab_lines, ping-bulk:~11807) but not on Bindings.

WANT: `[5 Bindings]` = full reference (as `[4 Commands]` has) followed by the
live table.

While there: the tab-content builder is a chain of `if self.help_tab == N`
with the fragment lists assembled inline per tab, and the same fragment
appears on several tabs by hand.  A tab -> fragments table would make
"which tab shows what" readable in one place, and would have made this gap
obvious.  Note `_build_tab_lines()` also re-shifts `_settings_line_map` by
`_HELP_PINNED` on every call and is called at least twice per interaction —
idempotent only because `_build_settings_lines` rebuilds the map from
scratch (already recorded under STILL OPEN).

## Log-file paths / banner (2026-08-18)
- _target_count is computed once in Application.__init__ from the caller's
  entries, so after an :edit reload that adds or removes hosts, a later log
  switch writes a banner with the old count. Cosmetic. Counting monitors
  instead is not a fix: the first line can be written while that list is
  still being filled (config-file :log + a hosts-file parse warning does it),
  which is why the entry count was chosen.
- --dump-simple-script writes ':log' already expanded, so a dumped script logs
  beside the original file rather than beside itself. Documented rather than
  fixed: $SCRIPT_DIR is gone by the time entries are dumped. Re-emitting it
  would mean tracking provenance per entry.
- ':log' has no way to say "beside the hosts file" at runtime — only a
  hosts-file line can use $SCRIPT_DIR, since expansion is a parse-time step.
  A ':log %D/x.log'-style runtime token was considered and rejected: '%'
  tokens are expanded per host at command-build time and a log path has no
  host, so it would need a second, incompatible expander inside _cmd_log.

