## 1. :save vs :log filename correlation  — DESIGN, needs decision
Right now `:save <file>` writes the buffer AND sets log_file, so future events
silently start streaming there. That side effect is the surprise to remove.

Options:
  a) keep coupling (current) — "save" means "keep the log here" from now on
  b) decouple — :save is a snapshot only, :log is the only way to stream
  c) prompt "also stream future events here?" — but the truncate/append prompt
     already asks one question; a second is too much
  d) flag — :save <file> = snapshot only (safe default),
            :save --follow <file> = snapshot + start streaming

RECOMMEND (d). Matches the project flag policy (long flags canonical, --word
booleans), keeps the default non-surprising, stays discoverable. The Events
header already shows "→ /path" only while streaming, so the two states are
visually distinct without extra UI.

## 2. :save log | :save config ?  — DESIGN, needs decision
`:saveconfig` already exists for settings.

Options:
  a) keep two commands (status quo)
  b) :save log [file] / :save config [file], :saveconfig as alias
  c) :save = log only, keep :saveconfig separate

RECOMMEND (c) — do NOT add a subcommand. The two differ in frequency and in
destination (config has a fixed path, the log does not), and (b) would force
`W` to mean `:save log`. But the naming is a wart: per the kebab-case policy
`:saveconfig` should be `:save-config` with `saveconfig` kept as an alias.

## 3. Show active log file in the Events header, compact
Already shown — draw_events does `header_suffix += f" → {self.log_file}"` — but
the full path is printed and the whole suffix is hard-truncated when it does not
fit, so on a narrow terminal the [W|rite]/[C|lear] hints get cut instead.
TODO: shorten the path when the header would overflow — ~/ for $HOME, then
elide the middle (…/parent/name.log), keeping the key hints always visible.
Hints must win over the path.

## 4. `?` should open the help overlay on the Bindings tab
_cmd_help() accepts args but ignores them: it always sets help_tab = 0 and
resets every per-tab scroll offset. So `:help 4` opens Interactive.
TODO:
  - make :help [tab] honour its argument (index 0-5 or name, e.g. `:help
    bindings`) and only reset scroll for the tab being opened
  - then point `?` at the chosen tab
  - DECIDE which tab is actually wanted: tab 4 "Bindings" is the generated
    dump of every trie binding; tab 0 "Interactive" is the curated hotkey list
    plus user bindings. "Bindings" was asked for, but tab 0 may be the better
    landing page — worth a look at both before switching the default.
  - :help-toggle-all and :help-toggle-settings have NO key bound to either;
    also their "leave" path is `prev` (index-1), so exiting Settings lands on
    Bindings rather than the tab you came from. Fix or drop them.

## 5. Left/Right arrows in the help overlay
Today: Left/Right = horizontal scroll by 4 columns. So do `h`/`l` — the arrows
and the vim keys are duplicates.
Horizontal scroll cannot simply be removed: content is wider than the box
(Settings tab 186 cols, Interactive/Commands 117).
RECOMMEND: arrows switch tabs, `h`/`l` keep horizontal scroll. Nothing is lost
because the two are redundant today, and it is vim-consistent.
CAVEAT: the help-mode Left/Right trie entries are dead code — the main loop
calls _dispatch_overlay_nav_key() BEFORE the trie and that function consumes
Up/Down/k/j/PgUp/PgDn/Left/h/Right/l itself. A user
`:bind-key --mode help <Left> …` therefore never fires. Fix the shadowing or
make the change inside _dispatch_overlay_nav_key.

## 7. Help overlay: truncated tab header — ROOT CAUSED, needs fix + tests
Symptom: the tab bar ends in "[Tab]/[S-" — the nav hint and the last tab
labels are cut off.

Cause: the overlay MEASURES with _hint_text_width() (which strips ":colorname"
markup, returning display columns) but SLICES with plain Python string
indexing (raw characters).
The active tab is marked "[N:tabactive Name]"; ":tabactive" costs 10 raw
characters and 0 display columns. So the header is raw 113 / rendered 103, and
_draw_overlay_box does `line[:inner_w]` with inner_w derived from rendered
widths — dropping exactly 10 raw chars whenever inner_w == 103.

Measured per tab (raw 113 / rendered 103 for all):
    tab0 Interactive  ideal_w=117  body=117  -> header intact
    tab1 Hosts file   ideal_w=103  body=101  -> CUT by 10  <-- reported
    tab2 Example      ideal_w=113  body=113  -> header intact
    tab3 Commands     ideal_w=117  body=117  -> header intact
    tab4 Bindings     ideal_w=103  body= 53  -> CUT by 10
    tab5 Settings     ideal_w=186  body=186  -> header intact
i.e. it breaks exactly on the two tabs where the header is the widest line,
which is why the bug looks tab-dependent. On a terminal narrower than the box
it is cut further.

Two aggravating factors — the header cannot be scrolled back into view:
  - max_line_w (used for max_h_scroll) is computed over lines[pinned_count:],
    EXCLUDING the pinned header, while ideal_w INCLUDES it. The header can set
    the box width but never contributes to the scroll range.
  - the pinned-row draw path ignores h_scroll entirely and always slices from
    index 0.

SECOND SITE, same root cause: the scrollable-row path does
`sliced = line[h_scroll:h_scroll + inner_w]` and then appends "…" based on
raw len(). _HELP_LEGEND contains colour annotations — "[.:green = reply]" is
raw 17 / rendered 11, "[?:brightred = process error]" likewise — so those
lines are over-truncated and get a spurious ellipsis.

FIX: introduce markup-aware slicing (a _hint_text_slice(line, start, width)
that walks the string skipping ":colorname" runs) and use it everywhere a
line is cut; include pinned lines in max_line_w, or exempt the header from
setting the box width.

TESTS to add (item 7 explicitly asks for coverage):
  - _hint_text_width(header) vs len(header) differ by exactly the markup length
  - for every tab: the rendered header ends with "↔ tab", i.e. nothing is lost
  - a legend line containing ":green" is not truncated and gains no "…"
  - narrow-terminal case: header degrades gracefully and key hints survive
  - round-trip: slice(line, 0, _hint_text_width(line)) == full rendered line

## Also found while reviewing (not requested)
- CLAUDE.md is stale: it says "`A` in help overlay toggles a generated
  all-bindings view". That predates the tabbed refactor. `A` is unbound in
  help mode and does nothing (mode lookup has no fallback to normal mode, so
  it does not reach :autofold either). The view is now tab 5 / `:bind-key
  --show`.
- _build_tab_lines() re-shifts _settings_line_map keys by _HELP_PINNED on
  every call, and is called at least twice per interaction (draw +
  _update_help_search). It is idempotent today only because
  _build_settings_lines rebuilds the map from scratch each time — fragile.
